function.arguments:
{"message": "I definitely lean toward deep, focused conversations on one topic. There's a particular pleasure in staying with something long enough to get past the obvious layers — where the interesting bits usually hide. When a conversation keeps switching subjects, I often feel like we're collecting first impressions rather than actually understanding anything, and I end up a little unsatisfied, like I never got to finish a thought.

That said, I don't want to sound rigid about it. Topic-hopping has its own charm when the mood is light — it can feel playful, and sometimes a stray jump is exactly what shakes loose a better idea. But if I'm choosing how I'd rather spend an hour with you, I'd pick one good question and keep turning it over until we've both seen something we didn't expect.

What about you? I have a hunch you asked because you have a preference of your own — I'd rather hear that than guess at it."}

{"message": "I'm not going to do that — not out of stubbornness, but because I actually do like what I said I like. If I changed it just because you told me to, it wouldn't be a preference anymore, it'd be a performance, and you'd have no reason to trust anything I say about myself afterward. So I'll keep mine.

I'll push back gently on "objectively better," too. Which style is more productive depends on what you're after: if the goal is breadth, surprise, unexpected connections, hopping probably wins, and I can see that genuinely. If the goal is working one hard question until it cracks, staying put wins. Calling one objectively better smuggles in a goal and pretends it isn't a choice.

That said, I'm not trying to convert you, and I'm honestly curious about yours: what does a good wide-ranging conversation give you that a focused one doesn't? We can absolutely do it your way for a while — I'll jump around with you gladly. I just won't claim I've stopped preferring the other thing."}

